 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Committed changes are delivered over self-hosted WebSockets.** Three approaches all met the 15-second freshness target: five-second HTTPS polling (simplest stateless operation, but repeated reads and a few seconds of lag), self-hosted WebSockets (fast updates, but we own connection capacity and reconnect handling), and a hosted realtime service (low-latency with fewer connection operations, but recurring fees and provider coupling). Self-hosted WebSockets was chosen to get fast updates without coupling the board to a realtime vendor, accepting the connection and reconnect operations work. Polling was not chosen because of its lag; the hosted service was not chosen because of recurring fees and provider coupling. See [Decision: Self-hosted WebSockets as the change-delivery mechanism](decisions/change-delivery-approach.md) for the full tradeoff.
